Reference reassignment refcount semantics
=========================================

When a `&T` variable is reassigned, the refcount of the old
target must decrement (potentially triggering free) and the
refcount of the new target must increment. Two patterns to
pin:

  - **Reassign to a fresh allocation:** `r = new T(...)`. The
    new target is owned (refcount 1), so the implementation
    must *not* extra-incr it. The old target's refcount must
    drop. If the bug is symmetric (no incr on new + no decr
    on old), both targets leak; if the bug is asymmetric
    (decr on old but incr on new is wrong), the old target
    double-frees.

  - **Reassign to a borrowed reference:** `r = s` where
    `s: &T` already exists. The new target's refcount must
    incr (now two bindings); the old target's must decr.
    Without the incr, when one binding goes out of scope
    the other becomes dangling.

  - **Self-assign:** `r = r` is a no-op semantically but
    must still be inc-then-dec to avoid use-after-free.
    A naive decr-then-incr drops r to 0 first and frees
    it before the incr runs.

These tests observe the behaviour through field reads
after the reassignment chain. If refcounting is wrong, the
reads land on freed memory and either trap or return
garbage.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct NraBox
        v: int

Reassign to fresh allocation
----------------------------

    #test
    test_reassign_to_fresh() -> unit
        var r: &NraBox = new NraBox(1)
        r = new NraBox(2)
        r = new NraBox(3)
        // Each assignment drops the previous target. We can only observe
        // the *final* binding, but if any prior dec-then-free corrupted
        // the heap, the read here would trap.
        assert(r.v == 3, s"r.v should be 3 after three fresh allocations, got ${r.v}")

Reassign to a borrowed reference
--------------------------------

    #test
    test_reassign_to_borrowed() -> unit
        val owner: &NraBox = new NraBox(42)
        var alias: &NraBox = new NraBox(7)
        // alias's old target (NraBox(7)) should be decr'd to 0 and freed;
        // owner's target (NraBox(42)) should be incr'd to 2 (owner + alias).
        alias = owner
        assert(alias.v == 42, s"alias.v should be 42 (= owner.v), got ${alias.v}")
        assert(owner.v == 42, s"owner.v should still be 42, got ${owner.v}")
        // Mutating through alias is visible through owner — they share storage.
        alias.v = 99
        assert(owner.v == 99, s"owner.v should be 99 after alias.v=99, got ${owner.v}")

Self-assign is safe
-------------------

If the implementation does decr-then-incr (instead of incr-then-decr or
"detect self and skip"), the refcount briefly drops to 0 and the buffer
is freed before being "kept alive" again. The post-assign read would
then read freed memory.

    #test
    test_self_assign_no_free() -> unit
        var r: &NraBox = new NraBox(123)
        r = r
        assert(r.v == 123, s"r.v should still be 123 after r=r, got ${r.v}")
        r.v = 456
        assert(r.v == 456, s"r.v should be 456 after mutation, got ${r.v}")

Chained reassignment through a helper
-------------------------------------

`bump(r: &NraBox) -> &NraBox = new NraBox(r.v + 1)` returns a fresh ref. The
caller's reassignment must inc the new (it's already owned from `new`)
and dec the old. Repeated chaining stresses the inc/dec balance.

    bump(r: &NraBox) -> &NraBox = new NraBox(r.v + 1)

    #test
    test_chained_fresh_assignments() -> unit
        var r: &NraBox = new NraBox(0)
        var i = 0
        while i < 50
            r = bump(r)
            i = i + 1
        assert(r.v == 50, s"r.v should be 50 after 50 bumps, got ${r.v}")
